id: pm-0elqjj
title: "The immutable history op vocabulary is ungoverned: 71 op literals across five naming conventions, with the same operation spelled two ways in one file, are written permanently with no enum, no contract, and no gate"
description: "Every mutating action writes an entry whose op field names the operation. That name is the primary key analytics, replay, redaction, compaction and any external verifier read to understand what happened. It is written into an append-only stream that the project promises never to alter. Nothing declares what those names may be.\n\nCensus measured on this repository at 2026.7.28:\n\n- 71 distinct op string literals exist in src. There is no enum, no union type, no constant table, and no contract entry for any of them. The only central reference is a projection format string.\n- 37 distinct op values are already permanently recorded across 40,343 entries in 2,155 streams.\n- At least five naming conventions are in simultaneous use: bare verbs (create, close, claim, release, append, delete, import, investigate); snake_case compounds (comment_add, files_add, docs_add, note_add, learning_add, history_compact, history_compact_baseline, plan_add_step, files_discover); a colon-namespaced snake form (gc:lock_remove, gc:checkpoint_remove, gc:transaction_journal_remove, init:ensure_dir, init:runtime_schema_file, reindex:embeddings, lock:release); a colon-namespaced kebab form (history:author-acknowledge, profile:apply-fields, profile:apply-statuses, profile:apply-types, schema:add-field, schema:add-status, schema:add-type, schema:apply-preset); a colon SUFFIX namespace (create:history, delete:history, import:history, restore:history, history_redact:history, history_repair:history); and one dotted form (pm.command).\n- Number agreement is inconsistent within one family: files_add, docs_add and tests_add are plural while note_add, comment_add and learning_add are singular, for the same add-an-annotation shape.\n- Proof that this is drift and not design: src/sdk/init.ts writes op \"init:type-preset\" at line 565 and op \"init:type_preset\" at line 1029. The same operation is spelled two ways in the same file. Whichever path runs, the spelling is immutable from that moment.\n- history:author-acknowledge is the only recorded op using the colon-kebab form, and 9 entries already carry it permanently.\n\nConsequence: any consumer of the immutable record - a replay tool, an external auditor, a metrics job, a future format reader across the decades the format claims to cover - must special-case an open-ended vocabulary that no gate constrains. A new op name cannot be reviewed against a rule because no rule is written down. The record is immutable, so every drifted name is permanent and the cost of the gap only accumulates.\n\nThis is the same class as the dependency-kind lexicon and the flag lexicon and the error-code catalog, one layer deeper: those govern surfaces a caller can re-read, this governs a record nobody can ever rewrite. Scope is the op vocabulary only. Entry presence is a different concern and stays with the history-completeness gate. Entry ordering identity stays with its own owner."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 1
tags[3]: core,governance,history
created_at: "2026-07-28T02:52:26.634Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-01T09:52:05.174Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 300
acceptance_criteria: "A single declared source enumerates every permitted history op name, and every write site derives its op from that source rather than a literal; A naming grammar for op names is written down and machine-checked, so a non-conforming op cannot reach a stream; The init type-preset duplicate spelling is resolved to one name and the historical entries carrying the retired spelling are documented as a known legacy alias rather than rewritten; The declared vocabulary is published through the runtime contract surface so an external replay or verification tool can enumerate it without reading source; A gate fails when a new op literal appears that is absent from the declared vocabulary"
